= Prospective Students
Prethesis, thesis, and internship projects -- how to work with me

~~~
{Please read this page before emailing me}
I receive many more requests than I have places. Emails that follow the checklist on this page
get a reply; one-line emails asking "please give me a topic" usually do not. This is not
personal -- it is simply that I cannot evaluate a request without knowing who you are and what
you want to work on.
~~~

== 1. What I look for

- *Curiosity and persistence.* You should enjoy finding out why something does not work.
- *Comfort with mathematics.* Linear algebra, probability, and basic optimization. You do not
  need to be an expert -- you need to be willing to become one.
- *Programming.* Python (NumPy/SciPy) or MATLAB for simulations. Git for version control.
- *Reading.* You will read research papers, and you will write summaries of them.
- *Reliability.* Attending meetings, meeting agreed deadlines, and telling me early when
  something is stuck.

== 2. The prethesis / thesis process

- *Step 1 -- Choose a topic.* Pick one of the topics in
  section 3 below, or propose your own related to the group's work.
- *Step 2 -- Talk to me.* Send the email described in section 4. If we agree, we fix the
  scope in writing (one page: goal, deliverables, timeline).
- *Step 3 -- Prethesis (one semester).* Literature review, a concrete formulation, and a
  first simulation or proof-of-concept. Deliverable: a short technical report.
- *Step 4 -- Thesis (one to two semesters).* Full development, experiments, and a written
  thesis. Deliverable: thesis document and, if the results allow, a conference or journal
  paper.
- *Step 5 -- Presentation and defence.* A group seminar before submission, then the formal
  defence.

== 3. Currently available topics

The list below is updated every semester. Topics marked *(open)* are available now.

=== T1. Learning-based power control for interference-limited networks (open)
*Question:* can a learned policy approach the performance of iterative optimization while
running in real time?\n
*Scope:* reproduce a baseline weighted-MMSE scheme, then train a graph neural network policy on
random network instances and compare sum-rate and runtime.\n
*Prerequisites:* Python, PyTorch (or willingness to learn), basic probability.\n
*Good outcome:* a reproducible simulation study, and a comparison that is honest about where
learning wins and where it does not.

=== T2. Reconfigurable intelligent surfaces with imperfect channel knowledge (open)
*Question:* how much of the theoretical gain of an RIS survives when the channel is only known
approximately?\n
*Scope:* formulate a robust beamforming problem, derive a tractable approximation, and quantify
the performance loss as a function of channel estimation error.\n
*Prerequisites:* convex optimization basics, MATLAB or Python.\n
*Good outcome:* a clear robustness/performance trade-off curve plus a short write-up.

=== T3. Energy efficiency and delay in dense small-cell networks (open)
*Question:* what is the price, in energy, of guaranteeing a delay constraint?\n
*Scope:* build on queueing models, derive a scheduling policy, and evaluate the energy-delay
trade-off by simulation.\n
*Prerequisites:* queueing basics, simulation experience.\n
*Good outcome:* a policy with a provable guarantee plus simulations.

=== T4. Propose your own topic (open)
If you have read a paper that excited you, write a one-page proposal: the problem, why it
matters, what you would do, and what you would deliver. I am happy to discuss it.

== 4. How to apply

Send one email to [phuong.luuvo@example.edu]. Start the subject line with the kind of project
you are applying for -- *Prethesis*, *Thesis*, or *Internship* -- followed by your full name,
for example *Prethesis - Nguyen Van A*.

and include:

- Your CV (one page is enough).
- Your transcript (an unofficial PDF is fine).
- The topic number you are interested in (for example *T2*), or your own one-page proposal.
- Two or three sentences on why this topic interests *you* specifically.
- Your availability: which semester, and how many hours per week.

== 5. Frequently asked questions

~~~
{Frequently asked questions}
*Do I need to have published anything?* No. Enthusiasm plus solid fundamentals is enough.

*Do I need to be in the top of my class?* Not necessarily -- but you must be able to handle
the mathematics in the papers you will read.

*Can I work remotely?* Partly, but the initial meetings should be in person.

*How long until I get a reply?* Usually within one or two weeks during term time. If you have
not heard back after two weeks, a short reminder is welcome.

*Can you write me a recommendation letter?* Yes, if we have worked together for at least one
semester -- I can only write about what I have seen.
~~~

